  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    I'm Vish. Sydney-based, senior estimator by day, side-project tinkerer by
    night, occasional reader of things that won't pass an algorithm test. I
    price construction work for a living and build software in the gaps. This
    site is the bit of the internet I own.
  </p>

  <h2>The day job</h2>
  <p>
    Senior estimator in retail and commercial fit-out. Tenders for shopfitters
    and fit-out contractors building stores for premium retail and hospitality
    clients. The work is half maths, half judgement, and increasingly half AI
    tooling I build myself. The CV-shaped version sits at <a href="/work">/work</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The notebook</h2>
  <p>
    I take notes the way some people take photographs — constantly, mostly
    badly, occasionally one's worth keeping. Atomic ideas, lessons from
    tenders won and lost, takes I used to hold and don't anymore. The good
    ones become essays at <a href="/notes">/notes</a>. The rest live in a
    private vault and that's exactly where they belong.
  </p>

  <h2>The build pile</h2>
  <p>
    A small portfolio of side projects, each useful to me first: scope-generation
    tooling for estimators, a roguelike submarine game, a portable AI assistant
    for construction sites. Most of them won't ship. The point of side projects
    isn't shipping. The point is the practice and the unreasonable amount you
    learn building things you actually care about.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond the day job</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/prepping">Prepping</a> — the boring kind. Redundancy, open-source tools, single-point-of-failure removal.</li>
    <li><a href="/music">Music</a> — playing guitar and piano, slowly working out music production.</li>
    <li><a href="/movies">Movies</a> — what holds up, what doesn't, why.</li>
  </ul>

